+# vxn backend (Xen DomU isolation)
+
+The `vxn` sandbox backend runs an AXIS-sandboxed command inside a **Xen DomU** —
+a small VM with its own kernel — instead of as an isolated host process. Where
+the native backend confines a process on the host kernel (Landlock + seccomp +
+namespaces), vxn puts a whole VM boundary around the workload: a guest-kernel
+exploit cannot reach the host, and denied resources simply do not exist in the
+guest. It is selected with `axis run --policy vxn` (or any policy whose
+`runtime.provider` is `vxn`).
+
+> **Important:** the AXIS-native process primitives (seccomp-BPF, Landlock,
+> netns, cgroups) are kernel-local and do **not** cross into the DomU's separate
+> kernel, so they are not applied inside the guest. The VM boundary itself
+> enforces the big-ticket policy (filesystem isolation, network block, memory
+> cap, separate kernel). Fine-grained in-guest enforcement is a documented
+> future layer.
+
+## Prerequisite: a vxn SDK
+
+The vxn backend drives the **`vxn` CLI**, which ships in a *vxn SDK* built from
+the `meta-virtualization` Yocto layer. The SDK is a relocatable tarball bundling
+the `vxn` host CLI plus a Xen dom0 image that runs under QEMU (KVM-accelerated),
+so it works on any Linux host (or WSL2) with `/dev/kvm` — no Xen hardware
+needed.
+
+Build it yourself following meta-virtualization's **`docs/vxn-sdk.md`**
+(<https://git.yoctoproject.org/meta-virtualization/>, under `docs/`), or install
+a prebuilt SDK if one was provided to you. Then, in the shell where you run
+`axis`:
+
+```
+source <sdk-install-dir>/init-env.sh     # puts vxn / vxn-<arch> on PATH
+vxn-x86_64 run --rm alpine echo hi         # sanity-check the SDK itself first
+```
+
+If the sanity check works, AXIS can use the backend.
+
+## Running an agent under vxn
+
+```
+axis run --policy vxn -- claude
+```
+
+- The **image is derived from the command name** when `VXN_BASE_IMAGE` is unset
+  (`-- claude` → image `claude`), which vxn auto-provisions from its shipped
+  recipe on first run. Set `VXN_BASE_IMAGE` to run a command in a fixed base
+  image instead (`VXN_BASE_IMAGE=alpine axis run --policy vxn -- echo hi`).
+- Under a tty, `axis run` is interactive: the backend adds `-it`, and the
+  agent's TUI (e.g. claude's login/REPL) drives your real terminal.
+- The built-in `vxn` policy sets `network.mode: allow` so an interactive agent's
+  OAuth/API calls work out of the box. Use a file policy with
+  `network.mode: block` for a no-egress run.
+
+## Environment knobs
+
+| Variable | Effect |
+|----------|--------|
+| `VXN_BIN` | vxn binary to invoke (default `vxn`). Set to the arch-suffixed `vxn-x86_64`, an absolute path, or the in-dom0 `vxn` (see config a/b below). |
+| `VXN_BASE_IMAGE` | Force the DomU image instead of deriving it from the command. |
+| `VXN_FORWARD_ENV` | Comma-separated host env var **names** to forward into the DomU. AXIS strips secrets from the collected sandbox env; this is the knowing opt-in to re-introduce e.g. `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` for a direct-key (non-proxy) run. Nothing is forwarded unless named here. |
+| `VXN_MEMORY` / `VXN_VCPUS` | Memory / vCPUs for the container DomU. |
+
+## Network modes
+
+The policy's `network.mode` maps to the DomU NIC:
+
+- `block` → `--no-network` (no vif at all — stronger than a netns)
+- `allow` → default bridge (DomU gets its own networking)
+- `proxy` → **fails closed**: vxn has no AXIS managed-inference path yet, and
+  silently downgrading strict-proxy to allow would violate the isolation
+  contract. Use `block` or `allow`.
+
+## config "a" vs config "b"
+
+- **config a (default):** the host `vxn` CLI boots and uses a nested dom0 under
+  QEMU and dispatches the container DomU. The backend just spawns `vxn run …`.
+  Nothing extra to set.
+- **config b:** AXIS and vxn run *inside* a real Xen dom0. Point `VXN_BIN` at the
+  in-dom0 `vxn` — no code change, same backend.
+
+## Porting to other host platforms
+
+Today the vxn backend is **Linux-only by design**: it lives in
+`crates/axis-sandbox/src/linux/vxn.rs`, is selected only as the Linux `LinuxVxn`
+backend, and `sandbox.rs` returns `Unsupported` for `provider: vxn` on Windows
+and other platforms. This section is what a port *would* take — it is not yet
+implemented.
+
+Two independent things must be true on the target host.
+
+**1. A `vxn` CLI on that host** (the backend spawns it; `VXN_BIN` points at it).
+The backend's contract with the CLI is small and OS-agnostic in shape:
+
+- `vxn run [-it] [--env-b64=…] [--policy-b64=…] <image> <argv…>` starts the sandbox;
+- the backend manages the child by pid / stdio / (pty for `-it`) / wait / kill.
+
+So "interface with a vxn running on that platform" means a `vxn` that speaks this
+same `run` interface and reaches a dom0. The `vxn` CLI itself is POSIX shell +
+`ssh` + the `xl` toolstack, so the host needs a POSIX environment.
+
+**2. The axis Rust selecting + driving it on that OS.** The code is nearly
+portable; the concrete Linux-isms are:
+
+- **Placement + gating** — the module is under `src/linux/` and `sandbox.rs`
+  hard-errors on non-Linux. Lift `VxnSandbox` to a shared location (or add the
+  target OS to the platform selection) and relax the guard.
+- **`libc::kill(pid, SIGTERM/SIGKILL)`** — the only OS-specific syscall (the
+  kill/timeout path). Replace with a portable stop: `Child::kill()` for the hard
+  kill, and a `#[cfg(unix)]` / `#[cfg(windows)]` split (or a small crate) for the
+  graceful SIGTERM-then-SIGKILL sequence.
+
+Everything else — `Command`, `Stdio`, the child stdio handles, the base64 /
+env / policy-blob building, image derivation — is already cross-platform.
+
+**Per platform:**
+
+- **macOS** — the tractable case. `bash` and `ssh` are native, so the `vxn` SDK
+  CLI runs as-is and the dom0 boots under QEMU (HVF). Work is the Rust changes
+  above (small, mechanical) plus verifying the CLI path.
+- **Other Unix (BSD, …)** — same shape as macOS: a POSIX shell + `ssh` are
+  present; the Rust port is identical.
+- **Windows** — the hard case, deliberately excluded today. There is no native
+  POSIX path for the `vxn` CLI (shell + ssh + xl), so it needs **WSL**: run the
+  `vxn` CLI inside WSL2 and point `VXN_BIN` at a wrapper that crosses into it (the
+  dom0 boots under WSL2 / Hyper-V / QEMU). This is why the code returns a
+  Windows-specific `Unsupported` message rather than the generic one.
+
+In short: the axis-side port is small — relocate the module, relax the guard, and
+swap the one `libc::kill` for a portable stop. The substantive requirement is a
+**POSIX `vxn` CLI on the host**: native on macOS/BSD, WSL on Windows.
+
